WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.315
Endpoint and system telemetry provides activity that network observation may not reveal.

00:00:05.315 --> 00:00:09.461
Engineering workstations, HMIs, application servers, historians, jump hosts,

00:00:09.461 --> 00:00:14.240
and remote-access systems can record users, processes, files, services, configurations,

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.455
and local events.

00:00:15.455 --> 00:00:19.100
Authentication records show successful and failed logons, account use, privilege,

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.160
and session timing.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.870
File telemetry may reveal project access, new executables, configuration changes,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.670
or removable-media activity.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.275
Service events can identify startup, shutdown, failure,

00:00:29.275 --> 00:00:32.525
or changes to applications that support control visibility.

00:00:32.525 --> 00:00:36.430
Engineering workstations are especially important because specialized tools can configure

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:37.160
controllers,

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.395
HMIs, networks, and field devices.

00:00:39.395 --> 00:00:43.095
Relevant records may include project versions, downloads, connection targets,

00:00:43.095 --> 00:00:45.000
firmware work, and change approvals.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.700
These actions need strong identity and maintenance context.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.375
HMI, historian, and SCADA servers can provide alarms, application errors, tag changes,

00:00:52.375 --> 00:00:54.400
database activity, and collection health.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.445
Remote-access gateways add source, destination, approval, session duration,

00:00:58.445 --> 00:01:02.585
and sometimes activity records that connect an external identity to an internal asset.

00:01:02.585 --> 00:01:05.100
Collection must be compatible with the supported system.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.760
An endpoint agent, audit setting,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.830
or aggressive log forwarding configuration can consume resources or disrupt a legacy

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:11.700
application.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.000
Test changes with engineering and vendors, monitor overhead, and define a rollback path.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.995
Normalize endpoint records with asset role, site, owner, and reliable time.

00:01:19.995 --> 00:01:23.900
Protect logs from unauthorized change and retain enough history for investigation.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.440
Cross-source correlation is strongest: a user logon, remote session,

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.700
engineering connection, controller change,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.110
and process event together provide more confidence than any one record.
